title the position words both parsers refuse

# Both parsers refuse these names, in the same words, and this file
# is in the cross-parser diff so that stays true. It used to be a
# real divergence -- the reference accepted them as aliases -- and
# then a wording difference; it is neither now.
#
# They are refused because they carry NO DIRECTION, not because they
# carry the wrong one. It is tempting to record that the old aliases
# were backwards, and that is the version to avoid: two people who
# had both read this project closely turned out to hold opposite
# mappings, and each found the same fixture plausible under their own
# reading. There was never a correct direction to preserve.
#
# That is how a mirrored fixture survives review. The reader supplies
# the mapping, so the document agrees with whoever is reading it --
# not merely plausible to a wrong reader, but affirmatively
# confirming. Hence delete the words rather than reverse them; the
# roles are onto and off-of, and positions cannot name them.
#
# The advice to write onto or offof belongs ONLY to these two names.
# A merely unknown name gets the generic message, or the advice
# becomes noise attached to every typo -- see err-unknown-tree.tree,
# which pins that and is diffed alongside this.

tree left
  1-1: /

tree right
  1-1: /
